.TH ZIG_STDLIB 7 "2026-10-06" "Zig 0.17.0" "Zig Manual"
.SH NAME
zig-stdlib \- overview of the Zig Standard Library
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Zig Standard Library contains commonly used algorithms, data
structures, and definitions to help build programs or libraries. It is
distributed with every Zig release and requires no external package
manager.
.PP
The library is organized as a single namespace, \fBstd\fR. Everything
lives under that root; there is no separate include path or linking
step. A program that uses any part of the Standard Library imports it
with:
.nf
    const std = @import("std");
.fi
.PP
The library is written in Zig and compiled together with the user's
code. This makes it available on freestanding targets, in operating
system kernels, and in embedded environments where no runtime exists.
.SH DOCUMENTATION
The Standard Library is documented separately from the language
reference. The upstream documentation is available at:
.PP
.RS 4
\fBhttps://ziglang.org/documentation/master/std/\fR
.RE
.PP
This page is an overview. It does not document individual functions or
types. Refer to the upstream documentation for the complete API.
.SH LOCAL DOCUMENTATION
A local, offline copy of the Standard Library documentation is
generated from the installed compiler. It can be served over a local
web server:
.nf
    zig std
.fi
.PP
This command starts a small HTTP server on a local port and prints the
URL to standard output. Open the URL in a web browser to read the
documentation. Press Ctrl-C to stop the server.
.PP
The documentation is generated from doc comments in the source. It
reflects the exact version of the Standard Library installed with the
Zig compiler being used.
.PP
The interface provides search (press \fBs\fR), navigation between
namespaces, and a shortcut to the source of every symbol (press
\fBu\fR). The viewer is a WebAssembly application; no network access is
required beyond the local server.
.SH STRUCTURE
The Standard Library is organized as nested namespaces under
\fBstd\fR. The following list describes the top-level modules that are
used most frequently. It is not exhaustive.
.SS Memory
.TP
\fBstd\.mem\fR
Byte and slice operations: \fBcopyForwards\fR, \fBcopyBackwards\fR,
\fBeql\fR, \fBindexOf\fR, \fBstartsWith\fR, \fBendsWith\fR, and the
\fBAllocator\fR interface. Also hosts \fBsentinel\fR, \fBtokenize\fR,
and \fBsplit\fR helpers.
.TP
\fBstd\.heap\fR
Concrete allocator implementations: \fBGeneralPurposeAllocator\fR,
\fBpage_allocator\fR, \fBc_allocator\fR, \fBSmpAllocator\fR,
\fBArenaAllocator\fR, \fBFixedBufferAllocator\fR.
.TP
\fBstd\.mem\.Allocator\fR
The vtable-based allocator interface. Every allocation site accepts an
\fBAllocator\fR value, either by pointer or by copy.
.SS Filesystem and process
.TP
\fBstd\.fs\fR
File and directory handling: \fBFile\fR, \fBDir\fR, \fBopenFile\fR,
\fBcreateFile\fR, \fBmakeDir\fR, \fBdeleteFile\fR, path resolution
(\fBrealpath\fR, \fBresolve\fR), and the \fBpath\fR sub-namespace.
.TP
\fBstd\.process\fR
Command-line arguments (\fBargsAlloc\fR), environment variables
(\fBgetEnvMap\fR), and child process spawning (\fBChild\fR,
\fBChild\.run\fR).
.TP
\fBstd\.os\fR
OS-level primitives that do not fit elsewhere: file descriptors,
system calls, and platform-specific constants.
.SS Input and output
.TP
\fBstd\.io\fR
Byte and buffered I/O. Provides \fBReader\fR, \fBWriter\fR, and the
associated \fBbufferedReader\fR and \fBbufferedWriter\fR adapters used
throughout the library.
.TP
\fBstd\.fmt\fR
Formatted printing and parsing: \fBformat\fR, \fBallocPrint\fR,
\fBparseInt\fR, \fBparseFloat\fR, and the \fBformat\fR method
convention. Format strings are parsed at compile time where the format
is comptime-known.
.SS Collections
.TP
\fBstd\.ArrayList\fR
Growable array. Offers \fBinit\fR, \fBappend\fR, \fBappendSlice\fR,
\fBpop\fR, \fBorderedRemove\fR, and \fBswapRemove\fR. Managed and
unmanaged variants both exist.
.TP
\fBstd\.HashMap\fR
Hash map with open addressing. \fBStringHashMap\fR, \fBAutoHashMap\fR,
and \fBHashMapUnmanaged\fR are specialized variants.
.TP
\fBstd\.ArrayHashMap\fR
Hash map that preserves insertion order. \fBStringArrayHashMap\fR is
the common specialization.
.TP
\fBstd\.BoundedArray\fR
Fixed-capacity array with a length field, stored inline.
.TP
\fBstd\.PriorityQueue\fR
Binary heap.
.TP
\fBstd\.RingBuffer\fR
Fixed-capacity FIFO queue.
.SS Text and data formats
.TP
\fBstd\.json\fR
JSON parsing and serialization. \fBparseFromSlice\fR,
\fBparseFromTokenSource\fR, \fBstringify\fR, and the streaming
\fBScanner\fR.
.TP
\fBstd\.unicode\fR
UTF-8 and UTF-16 encoding and decoding.
.TP
\fBstd\.base64\fR
Base64 encoding and decoding, standard and URL-safe alphabets.
.TP
\fBstd\.crypto\fR
Hashes (SHA-2, SHA-3, BLAKE3), MACs (HMAC, Poly1305), and
cryptographic primitives. Includes \fBstd\.crypto\.random\fR for
cryptographically secure randomness.
.TP
\fBstd\.compress\fR
Compression codecs: \fBflate\fR, \fBgzip\fR, \fBzlib\fR, \fBzstd\fR,
\fBlzma\fR, \fBxz\fR.
.SS Networking
.TP
\fBstd\.net\fR
TCP and UDP sockets, address parsing (\fBAddress\fR,
\fBAddress\.parseIp\fR), and DNS resolution.
.TP
\fBstd\.http\fR
Minimal HTTP client. Server implementations are outside the scope of
the Standard Library.
.SS Concurrency
.TP
\fBstd\.Thread\fR
Thread creation (\fBspawn\fR), \fBMutex\fR, \fBRwLock\fR,
\fBCondition\fR, and thread-local storage helpers.
.TP
\fBstd\.atomic\fR
Atomic operations with explicit memory ordering:
\fBAtomic\.load\fR, \fBAtomic\.store\fR, \fBAtomic\.rmw\fR,
\fBAtomic\.cmpxchg\fR.
.TP
\fBstd\.Thread\.Pool\fR
A simple work-stealing task pool.
.SS Numeric and utility
.TP
\fBstd\.math\fR
Numeric constants (\fBpi\fR, \fBe\fR), floating-point helpers,
\fBceil\fR, \fBfloor\fR, \fBabs\fR, \fBmin\fR, \fBmax\fR, and bit
manipulation functions.
.TP
\fBstd\.time\fR
Monotonic timestamps (\fBnanoTimestamp\fR, \fBmilliTimestamp\fR), the
\fBTimer\fR type, and calendar conversions in \fBstd\.time\.epoch\fR.
.TP
\fBstd\.testing\fR
Test utilities: \fBexpect\fR, \fBexpectEqual\fR, \fBexpectEqualStrings\fR,
\fBexpectError\fR, and \fBcheckAllAllocationFailures\fR for exercising
out-of-memory paths.
.TP
\fBstd\.debug\fR
Runtime debugging helpers: \fBassert\fR, \fBprint\fR,
\fBprintStackTrace\fR, and the \fBpanic\fR interface.
.TP
\fBstd\.log\fR
Structured logging with scoped levels.
.TP
\fBstd\.Build\fR
The build system API used by \fBbuild\.zig\fR. Not intended for use at
runtime.
.SH ALLOCATOR MODEL
The Standard Library does not have a default allocator. Any function
that allocates memory accepts an allocator parameter. This makes the
library usable in environments where no heap exists, and makes every
allocation explicit and traceable.
.PP
The allocator interface is \fBstd\.mem\.Allocator\fR. An allocator
value contains a pointer and a vtable with \fBalloca\fR, \fBresize\fR,
and \fBfree\fR methods. Any type that implements those methods can be
passed where an \fBAllocator\fR is expected.
.PP
Common allocators:
.TP
\fBGeneralPurposeAllocator\fR
Default choice for programs. Detects leaks and use-after-free when
safety is enabled. Configurable per-instance.
.TP
\fBpage_allocator\fR
Allocates whole pages from the operating system. No bookkeeping.
Suitable for backing larger structures.
.TP
\fBc_allocator\fR
Forwards to \fBmalloc\fR and \fBfree\fR from the C library. Available
only when linking libc.
.TP
\fBArenaAllocator\fR
Bump allocator that frees all allocations at once. Useful for parsing
and one-shot processing.
.TP
\fBFixedBufferAllocator\fR
Allocates from a caller-provided buffer. Useful for stack-allocated
scratch space and for tests.
.SH IO MODEL
The Standard Library exposes input and output through the \fBReader\fR
and \fBWriter\fR interfaces in \fBstd\.io\fR. Both follow the same
pattern as \fBAllocator\fR: a vtable plus a context pointer.
.PP
The standard streams are available as \fBstd\.io\.getStdOut\fR,
\fBstd\.io\.getStdErr\fR, and \fBstd\.io\.getStdIn\fR. Their return
type depends on the target and on whether libc is linked.
.PP
Buffered I/O is provided by \fBstd\.io\.bufferedReader\fR and
\fBstd\.io\.bufferedWriter\fR. Buffering is explicit; the library does
not buffer automatically.
.SH COMPTIME
Large parts of the Standard Library are usable in \fBcomptime\fR
contexts. Format strings, hash map parameters, and type constructors
are all evaluated at compile time when their inputs are known at
compile time.
.PP
For example, \fBstd\.fmt\.format\fR parses its format string during
semantic analysis, so a malformed format string is a compile error, not
a runtime failure.
.SH TESTING
The \fBstd\.testing\fR module provides the assertion helpers used by
the Standard Library's own test suite and by the Zig compiler's
\fBzig test\fR command.
.PP
Tests are declared with the \fBtest\fR keyword at file scope. The
\fBrefAllDecls\fR function forces analysis of every declaration in a
namespace, which is used to catch unused or unreferenced errors.
.SH FREESTANDING
On freestanding targets the Standard Library disables every module
that depends on a hosted operating system. Modules like \fBstd\.fs\fR,
\fBstd\.net\fR, and \fBstd\.process\fR are not available. Modules that
depend only on \fBstd\.mem\fR, \fBstd\.math\fR, \fBstd\.fmt\fR,
\fBstd\.json\fR, \fBstd\.crypto\fR, and \fBstd\.compress\fR remain
usable.
.PP
The exact set of available modules is determined by the builtin
\fB@import("builtin")\fR\.os\.tag and by the value of
\fB@import("builtin")\fR\.link_libc.
.SH VERSION NOTES
The following changes in Zig 0.17.0 affect the Standard Library.
.TP
\fBNew IO interface\fR
\fBstd\.io\fR has been reorganized around a new \fBReader\fR and
\fBWriter\fR interface. The old interfaces are being phased out.
.TP
\fB@cImport removed\fR
The \fB@cImport\fR builtin has been removed entirely. C headers must
now be translated with \fBtranslate-c\fR or by writing bindings by
hand.
.TP
\fB@intFromEnum and @enumFromInt replaced\fR
Replaced by \fB@backingInt\fR and \fB@fromBackingInt\fR. Code that
uses the old builtins must be updated.
.TP
\fBArrayList changes\fR
The interface of \fBArrayList\fR has been adjusted; both managed and
unmanaged variants now share a common implementation. See the
upstream release notes for the exact API.
.TP
\fBArray multiplication removed\fR
Initialization of arrays with the \fB**\fR operator has been removed.
Use \fB@splat\fR or an explicit loop.
